import Foundation

// MARK: - Service Content Expansion (Entries 7–20)

extension ContentLibrary {

    static let serviceContentExpansion: [DayContent] = [
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Mark 10:45",
            scriptureText: "For even the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Servant King",
            devotionalText: "If the Son of God came to serve, what does that say about you and me? Jesus redefined greatness — it's not about who has the most power, but who serves the most faithfully. His ultimate act of service was giving His life. You probably won't be asked for that, but you are asked to lay down your preferences, your comfort, and your schedule for the sake of others.",
            prayerText: "Lord Jesus, You came not to be served but to serve. Shape my heart to look like Yours. Help me see serving others not as beneath me but as the highest calling. Show me where I can give today — my time, my energy, my attention. I want to follow Your example of selfless love. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "In what area of your life are you more focused on being served than serving?",
            actionSteps: ["Do one task today that someone else normally does — without being asked", "Reflect on how Jesus served and identify one way to imitate Him this week"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Galatians 5:13",
            scriptureText: "You, my brothers and sisters, were called to be free. But do not use your freedom to indulge the flesh; rather, serve one another humbly in love.",
            devotionalTitle: "Freedom to Serve",
            devotionalText: "Freedom in Christ isn't freedom to do whatever you want — it's freedom to love sacrificially. Before Christ, you were enslaved to sin. Now you're free to choose service over selfishness. Humble service is the highest expression of Christian freedom. It's the opposite of what the world expects, and it's exactly what the world needs to see.",
            prayerText: "Lord, thank You for setting me free. Help me use my freedom not for selfish gain but for humble service. Where I'm tempted to indulge myself, redirect me toward loving others. Let my freedom be expressed through generosity, kindness, and selflessness. I serve because You first served me. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "How are you using the freedom Christ gave you — for yourself or for others?",
            actionSteps: ["Identify one way you can serve someone in your household today", "Ask someone 'How can I help you this week?' and follow through"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Matthew 25:35-40",
            scriptureText: "For I was hungry and you gave me something to eat, I was thirsty and you gave me something to drink, I was a stranger and you invited me in... The King will reply, 'Truly I tell you, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers and sisters of mine, you did for me.'",
            devotionalTitle: "Serving the Least",
            devotionalText: "Jesus identifies so closely with the vulnerable that serving them is serving Him. When you feed the hungry, welcome the stranger, or visit the sick, you're not just doing a good deed — you're encountering Christ. This changes everything about how we see service. The person in need isn't a project; they're an opportunity to meet Jesus face to face.",
            prayerText: "Lord, open my eyes to see You in the faces of the hungry, the lonely, and the forgotten. Help me serve the least of these — not out of obligation, but because I'm serving You. Give me compassion that leads to action. Let me never walk past a need I can meet. Whatever I do for the least, I do for You. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Who are 'the least of these' in your community, and how can you serve them?",
            actionSteps: ["Donate food, clothing, or time to a local shelter or food bank", "Look for one 'invisible' person today — someone often overlooked — and serve them"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "John 13:14-15",
            scriptureText: "Now that I, your Lord and Teacher, have washed your feet, you also should wash one another's feet. I have set you an example that you should do as I have done for you.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Towel and the Basin",
            devotionalText: "Jesus — the Lord of the universe — knelt down and washed dirty feet. It was the job of the lowest servant, and He did it willingly. Then He said, 'Do as I have done.' Service isn't optional for followers of Jesus. The towel and basin are as essential to your faith as your Bible and prayer. Today, look for feet to wash — metaphorically speaking.",
            prayerText: "Jesus, You knelt and served. Help me kneel too. Give me the humility to do the unglamorous work — the tasks no one sees, the roles no one applauds. I want to follow Your example, not just admire it. Show me whose feet need washing today. Let me serve with the same love and humility You showed Your disciples. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What is the 'foot washing' equivalent in your daily life — the humble service you tend to avoid?",
            actionSteps: ["Do one humble, unglamorous task for someone today without expecting recognition", "Read John 13:1-17 and journal about what it means for your life"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "1 Peter 4:10",
            scriptureText: "Each of you should use whatever gift you have received to serve others, as faithful stewards of God's grace in its various forms.",
            devotionalTitle: "Gifted to Serve",
            devotionalText: "Your gifts aren't for your résumé — they're for other people. Every talent, skill, and ability you have is a form of God's grace entrusted to you. Being a faithful steward means using what you've been given for others, not hoarding it for yourself. You don't need to discover some hidden, mystical gift. Start with what you're good at and use it to bless someone.",
            prayerText: "Lord, thank You for the gifts You've given me. Help me see them as tools for serving others, not trophies for my shelf. Show me how to be a faithful steward of Your grace. I want to use every ability, every resource, every talent for Your purposes. Let my gifts bring glory to You and blessing to others. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What gifts has God given you that you haven't yet used to serve others?",
            actionSteps: ["List three things you're good at and brainstorm how each could serve someone", "Use one of your gifts to help someone this week"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Philippians 2:5-7",
            scriptureText: "In your relationships with one another, have the same mindset as Christ Jesus: Who, being in very nature God, did not consider equality with God something to be used to his own advantage; rather, he made himself nothing by taking the very nature of a servant.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Mindset of a Servant",
            devotionalText: "Jesus had every right to demand service. He was God. Instead, He emptied Himself and became a servant. Paul says to have the same mindset. That means choosing to lower yourself when you could leverage your position, choosing to serve when you could sit back. The servant mindset isn't natural — it's supernatural. It requires the Holy Spirit to reshape your default settings.",
            prayerText: "Lord Jesus, give me Your mindset. Where I want to be served, teach me to serve. Where I want recognition, help me embrace hiddenness. You made Yourself nothing for my sake. Help me empty myself for the sake of others. Transform my thinking until service becomes my first instinct, not my last resort. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Where do you tend to leverage your position instead of serving from it?",
            actionSteps: ["In your next interaction, consciously choose to serve rather than be served", "Ask the Holy Spirit to reveal one area where your mindset needs to shift toward servanthood"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Colossians 3:23-24",
            scriptureText: "Whatever you do, work at it with all your heart, as working for the Lord, not for human masters, since you know that you will receive an inheritance from the Lord as a reward. It is the Lord Christ you are serving.",
            devotionalTitle: "Working for an Audience of One",
            devotionalText: "Every task — from the mundane to the meaningful — is an opportunity to serve the Lord. When you change a diaper, finish a report, clean a kitchen, or mow a lawn, you're serving Christ. This perspective transforms drudgery into worship. You're not working for a boss, a spouse, or a paycheck. You're working for the Lord. And He sees every effort.",
            prayerText: "Lord, help me work at everything with all my heart — as if I'm serving You directly, because I am. Transform my attitude about mundane tasks. Let me see every responsibility as an offering. I know You see what no one else notices. I work for Your approval, not human applause. Let my work ethic be a testimony of Your faithfulness. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "How would your attitude toward your daily tasks change if you truly believed you were serving Jesus through them?",
            actionSteps: ["Choose your least favorite task today and do it wholeheartedly for the Lord", "Write 'For the Lord' on a sticky note and put it where you'll see it during work"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Isaiah 58:10",
            scriptureText: "And if you spend yourselves in behalf of the hungry and satisfy the needs of the oppressed, then your light will rise in the darkness, and your night will become like the noonday.",
            devotionalTitle: "Your Light Rises Through Service",
            devotionalText: "Isaiah reveals a beautiful exchange: when you spend yourself for others, your own darkness lifts. Service isn't just good for the people you help — it's healing for you. When you step outside your own problems to meet someone else's needs, something shifts in your soul. Your light rises. Your night becomes noonday. Generosity toward others often becomes the remedy for your own heaviness.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I want to spend myself for others. When I'm tempted to focus only on my own struggles, redirect my eyes toward those in greater need. As I serve the hungry and oppressed, let my own light rise. Turn my darkness into noonday through the power of selfless love. Use my service to heal both others and me. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Have you ever experienced personal healing or breakthrough through serving someone else?",
            actionSteps: ["Find one practical way to serve someone in need today — even if you're going through a hard time yourself", "Research a local organization serving the oppressed and volunteer or donate"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Galatians 6:9-10",
            scriptureText: "Let us not become weary in doing good, for at the proper time we will reap a harvest if we do not give up. Therefore, as we have opportunity, let us do good to all people, especially to those who belong to the family of believers.",
            devotionalTitle: "Don't Give Up Doing Good",
            devotionalText: "Service fatigue is real. You give and give and sometimes see no results. Paul knows this, and his encouragement is simple: don't quit. The harvest is coming. The impact of your faithful service may not be visible yet, but God sees every act of kindness, every sacrifice, every moment you chose others over yourself. Keep going. The harvest is worth the wait.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I confess I sometimes grow weary of doing good. Renew my strength and my motivation. Help me keep serving even when I don't see results. I trust that You are preparing a harvest from every seed I plant. Give me fresh energy and a long-term perspective. I won't give up because You never give up on me. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Where are you feeling weary in doing good, and what would help you keep going?",
            actionSteps: ["If you're feeling burned out from serving, take a moment to rest and ask God to renew your strength", "Do one small act of good today — even if it feels insignificant"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "James 2:15-17",
            scriptureText: "Suppose a brother or a sister is without clothes and daily food. If one of you says to them, 'Go in peace; keep warm and well fed,' but does nothing about their physical needs, what good is it? In the same way, faith by itself, if it is not accompanied by action, is dead.",
            devotionalTitle: "Faith That Acts",
            devotionalText: "James is blunt: faith without action is dead. It's not enough to feel compassion — you have to do something. Good intentions don't feed the hungry or clothe the naked. Service is where your faith becomes tangible, visible, and real. Today, don't just think about helping someone. Do it. Let your faith come alive through your hands and feet.",
            prayerText: "Lord, don't let my faith be dead. Move me from good intentions to good actions. When I see a need, give me the courage and generosity to meet it. I don't want to just say 'I'll pray for you' when I can also act. Let my faith be alive — visible, tangible, and active. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there a need you've noticed but haven't acted on? What's holding you back?",
            actionSteps: ["Identify one need you've been aware of and take concrete action to help today", "Give something tangible to someone in need — food, clothing, money, or time"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Hebrews 6:10",
            scriptureText: "God is not unjust; he will not forget your work and the love you have shown him as you have helped his people and continue to help them.",
            devotionalTitle: "God Remembers Your Service",
            devotionalText: "Sometimes service feels thankless. Nobody noticed. Nobody said thank you. Nobody acknowledged your sacrifice. But God noticed. He doesn't forget a single act of love shown to His people. Every unseen sacrifice, every quiet act of service, every moment you chose to help — God remembers it all. You may serve in obscurity, but you are never unnoticed by the One who matters most.",
            prayerText: "Lord, thank You for seeing what no one else sees. Thank You for remembering every act of service, even the ones the world forgets. Help me serve without needing applause, knowing that Your approval is enough. I trust that You are not unjust — You will honor faithfulness in Your time and in Your way. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Does knowing that God remembers every act of service change your motivation to serve?",
            actionSteps: ["Do something kind for someone today that only God will see", "Write down three ways you've served recently and thank God that He remembers each one"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Acts 20:35",
            scriptureText: "In everything I did, I showed you that by this kind of hard work we must help the weak, remembering the words the Lord Jesus himself said: 'It is more blessed to give than to receive.'",
            devotionalTitle: "The Blessing of Giving",
            devotionalText: "These are Jesus' own words, preserved only here in Acts: it is more blessed to give than to receive. This isn't a guilt trip — it's an invitation to discover a deeper joy. Receiving is wonderful, but giving unlocks something in your soul that receiving never can. When you serve, give, and help the weak, you experience a blessing that money can't buy and the world can't take away.",
            prayerText: "Lord Jesus, I believe Your words — it truly is more blessed to give than to receive. Free me from the grip of selfishness. Give me a generous spirit that finds joy in giving, serving, and helping the weak. Let me experience the unique blessing that comes only through open-handed living. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "When was the last time you experienced the truth that giving is more blessed than receiving?",
            actionSteps: ["Give something away today that costs you something — time, money, or comfort", "Help someone who is weak or struggling, expecting nothing in return"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Proverbs 19:17",
            scriptureText: "Whoever is kind to the poor lends to the Lord, and he will reward them for what they have done.",
            devotionalTitle: "Lending to the Lord",
            devotionalText: "What an astonishing idea: when you're kind to the poor, God considers it a personal loan. The Creator of the universe puts Himself in your debt. Not because He needs your money, but because He values compassion so highly that He promises to personally repay it. Every act of generosity toward those in need is an investment with guaranteed returns from the most trustworthy source in existence.",
            prayerText: "Lord, open my eyes to the poor around me — not just the financially poor, but the emotionally poor, the spiritually poor, the relationally poor. Help me be kind in ways that make a real difference. I trust Your promise to reward generosity. I don't give to get, but I'm grateful that You honor every act of kindness. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "How does knowing that kindness to the poor is a 'loan to the Lord' change your perspective on generosity?",
            actionSteps: ["Find one way to be kind to someone in a difficult situation today", "Set aside money, time, or resources specifically for helping someone in need this week"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Matthew 20:26-28",
            scriptureText: "Instead, whoever wants to become great among you must be your servant, and whoever wants to be first must be your slave — just as the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many.",
            devotionalTitle: "Greatness Redefined",
            devotionalText: "The world says greatness means climbing to the top. Jesus says greatness means kneeling at the bottom. His disciples argued about who was the greatest, and Jesus flipped the script entirely. Want to be great? Serve. Want to be first? Go last. This isn't a clever paradox — it's the economy of God's kingdom. The greatest people in heaven will be the greatest servants on earth.",
            prayerText: "Lord, redefine greatness in my heart. Remove my desire to be noticed, promoted, or praised. Replace it with a desire to serve, to kneel, to wash feet. I want to be great in Your kingdom — and that means becoming a servant. Help me find joy in the lowest place, knowing that's where You meet me. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "By Jesus' definition of greatness, how great are you?",
            actionSteps: ["Choose to take the lowest position in a situation today — let someone else go first", "Serve someone in a way that no one will know about except you and God"]
        ),
    ]
}
